\begin{proof}

\leanok
The candidate Doob--Meyer decomposition of \(B_t^2\) is
\[
  B_t^2 = (B_t^2-t) + t.
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:martingale_brownian_sq_sub_time}, the first summand is a
martingale, hence a local martingale, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:isCadlag_brownian_sq_sub_time} it has càdlàg paths.  The second
summand is the deterministic time process \(T\).  The preceding deterministic
lemmas show that \(T\) is strongly predictable, strongly progressive, càdlàg,
has locally integrable running supremum, is nondecreasing pathwise, and starts
from zero.

The squared Brownian process used by the generic quadratic-variation
definition is \(X_t=\|B_t\|^2=B_t^2\).  The displayed identity is therefore a
normalized Doob--Meyer decomposition of \(X\), with martingale part
\(B_t^2-t\) and predictable part \(T_t=t\).  Applying the normalized
predictable-part comparison theorem identifies the choice-based predictable
part used in \(\operatorname{brownianQuadraticVariation}\) with \(T\) at the
fixed time \(t\), almost surely.

\end{proof}
